====================================================================
                            SetIterator
====================================================================

Class Description
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The 'SetIterator' class implements a custom iterator for traversing the elements
stored in the 'ArraySet'. It implements Java's 'Iterator' interface, allowing the
set to be accessed one element at a time, from the first element to the last.

The class keeps track of the current position and the number of elements to determine
when the iteration is complete.
Type Parameter: 'E': The type of elements stored in the set.

Attributes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    - **elements: The array containing the elements being traversed.
    - **current: The index of the next element to be returned.
    - **size: The total number of active elements available for iteration.

Methods
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    1. Constructor: SetIterator(E[] elements, int size) : Initializes the iterator with the array
                                                          and the number of active elements. The current index starts at zero.
                                                          Throws 'IllegalArgumentException' if the array is null or the size is negative.

    2. hasNext(): Checks whether another element is available in the array. Returns 'true' if more elements are available;
                    otherwise, 'false'.

    3. next(): Returns the next element in the iteration and advances the current index. Returns the next element in the array. Throws 'NoSuchElementException'
               if no more elements are available.

Purpose
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The purpose of this class is to provide sequential access to the elements stored in the set. It allows `ArraySet` to support iteration through an enhanced for-loop without exposing the internal array directly.